    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8049b986ebf95cd3c5edb566b3af6c72eb86dfb9398932a1371346a3282bab8f",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The quadratic two-body term is the compression mechanism: varying f(K*f) turns pair-scattering data into convolution feedback on a single pseudo-energy, producing a self-consistent thermal fixed point and its associated free-energy shift. The kernel representation preserves momentum-resolved pair influence and provides an algebraically direct interface for later choices of K, while dropping the organization and effects of forward n-particle terms with n at least 3. Because the span gives neither a small parameter nor a truncation-error bound, the resulting thermodynamics is conditional on the two-body approximation rather than controlled by the operator form itself.",
